<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Only the update downloads are exposed.

    A FileProvider that was given a broad path - "everything under the app's
    own files" - would let any app that could name our authority read the
    user's downloaded releases. This exposes one subdirectory of the app's
    private storage, and it holds nothing but APKs this app downloaded from the
    project's own GitHub releases.

    `files-path`, not `cache-path`, and this line is load-bearing in a way that
    is invisible until it is wrong.

    The two name different directories. The cache is the one Android is free to
    empty whenever it likes, and a downloaded APK must outlive the trip to
    Settings where the user grants the install permission - so #61 moved these
    downloads into the app's private files storage. This entry did not follow
    them: the provider was still publishing an empty directory under the cache.

    Nothing reports that at build time. `FileProvider.getUriForFile` throws at
    *runtime*, on the downloaded APK, the first time the user presses Install;
    `openInstaller` swallows the throw and reports that nothing opened; and the
    updater says "This device has no app installer" - on a phone with a
    perfectly working package installer, where the user is told to report a bug
    against a device that does not exist. Every update on every device failed
    that way, and the download itself was fine.

    `UpdateFileProviderPathsTest` reads this file and compares it with the
    directory `UpdateManager` actually writes to, so the two cannot drift apart
    again without a test going red.
-->
<paths>
    <files-path
        name="updates"
        path="updates/" />
</paths>